{"Vatican City":{"id":"2d1225a8-e051-48cf-81fe-9b4d4b5af891","word":"Vatican City","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"state — see also Vatican","tags":["feminine"],"word":"Cidade do Vaticano"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"state — see also Vatican","tags":["masculine"],"word":"Vaticano"}],"pos":"name","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"“The new State, which will be known as the Vatican City, has a tiny territory,” the Pope [Pius XI] said, “but it is the greatest in the world because it contains Bernig’s^([sic]) colonnades, Michelangelo’s dome, treasures of art in the Vatican library, museums and galleries, and because, above all, it contains the tomb of the Prince of the Apostles. No more precious territory exists.”","ref":"1929 February 12, Arnaldo Cortesi, “Pope’s Sovereignty Is Restored by Italo-Vatican Compact”, in The New York Times, volume LXXVIII, number 25,952, New York, N.Y.: The New York Times Company, →ISSN, →OCLC, archived from the original on 08 Aug 2025, page 2, column 2:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[43,55]],"id":"220a7e61-e351-42ed-bfad-9f35aeebd7da"},{"text":"Unlike most other countries in the world, citizenship in Vatican City isn’t solely provided to people that are born in the country. As there are no hospitals in the Vatican State, virtually no one is born in there. Instead, Vatican citizenship is provided on a ‘jus officii’ basis.","ref":"2019 October 3, Ellen Curtin, “Everything You Wanted to Know about Vatican City”, in City Wonders, archived from the original on 13 Jun 2025:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[57,69]],"id":"af449cce-2617-4e31-9d68-f1bb2276a8c5"},{"text":"The advisory mentions “travel to the Vatican City,” but did not specify if he would meet with the pope.","ref":"2024 May 6, Dana Rubinstein, William K. Rashbaum, “Mayor [Eric] Adams to Meet With Pope Francis in Rome”, in The New York Times, New York, N.Y.: The New York Times Company, →IS